00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.130
PRIYANKA: Hi, I'm
Priyanka [INAUDIBLE],,

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.620
and this is AI
Simplified, where we

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.140
learn to make our data useful.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.200
In this video, we're going
to imagine a company that

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.630
has large amount of
data which they want

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.420
to use to make
meaningful predictions,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.980
and take their business
to the next level.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.300
And we also know that their
team has varying levels

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.070
of machine learning expertise.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.170
To accelerate this
AI innovation,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.660
they are looking
at Vertex AI, which

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.860
provides tools for every step
of the machine learning workflow

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.920
from managing data sets to
different ways of training

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.140
the model,
evaluating, deploying,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.760
and making predictions.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.880
In this episode, let's
see the first step

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.920
of the machine
learning lifecycle,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.660
which is the data sets.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.700
You don't need to
worry about data sets.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.740
If your use case
is generic enough,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.080
you can just take advantage
of the pre-trained machine

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.070
learning APIs.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.610
And to learn more about them,
I've included a link below.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.280
In order to create custom
machine learning models,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.860
we must first have
a collection of data

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.570
to train our models with.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.210
That is what data sets are for.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.670
Data sets make data discoverable
from a central place.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.020
They provide the ability to
annotate and label the data

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.730
right from within the UI.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.250
You can track the lineage
for data governance

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.850
and can compare model
metrics between the models.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.780
Now, to create a data set,
we first select our data

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.500
and upload it, import it,
into the managed data sets.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.990
And then we can make
modifications and use

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.600
it to start training the model.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.170
Currently, there are four
supported data types--

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.710
image, tabular,
text, and videos.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.510
Now, image data sets currently
support image classification,

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.930
which is where models
predict one or many labels

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:49.890
from an image.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.560
Now, for example,
identifying the types of dog

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.440
treats from images of treats.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.060
Object detection is where the
model draws bounding boxes

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.380
around the items of the image.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.070
For example, you're
identifying the location

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:09.120
of different vegetables
within a picture of salad.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.240
Image segmentation
is where the model

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.310
can assign a label to pixel
level regions in an image.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.100
To ensure your model
performs well in production

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.680
and there's no
training serving skew,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.660
make sure the
training images are

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.120
close to the input
your users will

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.170
send to your deployed model.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.370
For example, if your images
are going to be low resolution,

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.910
be sure to have blurry
images, low resolution images,

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:36.810
in there.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.300
Include multiple
angles, backgrounds,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.340
and all sorts of resolutions.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.150
It's recommended that you
include at least 1,000 images

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.780
per label.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.760
But, you can always get
started with 10 per label.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.960
The more examples you provide,
the better your model will be.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.050
Now, tabular data sets
currently support regression,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.030
where you predict a numerical
value, and classification,

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.730
where you predict a
category associated

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.480
with a particular example.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.070
Forecasting is where the
model predicts likelihood

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.320
of sudden events or demands.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.830
For example, you want to predict
sales over a period of time.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.100
Now, tabular data
sets support hundreds

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.800
of columns and millions of rows.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.490
Text data sets currently
support classification,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.610
which is where you
assign one or more labels

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.680
to an entire document.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.470
In a customer support
example, an email

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.820
could include classifications as
great service or a suggestion.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:43.140
Now, entity extraction
is where the model

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.890
identifies custom text
entities within a document.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.490
For example, maybe you want
to identify all the phrases

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.940
in a customer support email
that reference pricing.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.670
Too expensive or great value.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.280
Sentiment analysis
is to understand

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.160
the overall sentiment
expressed in a block of text.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:08.460
For example, if a customer was
happy or upset or frustrated.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.380
Now, will your
data sets currently

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.100
support classification, which
provides labeled predictions

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.430
for an entire video,
shots, and frames?

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.680
For example, in a
football game, you

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.590
would be able to label the
section with commercials

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.580
and the section with the game.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.540
Now, action recognition
is where the model

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.620
identifies clips in the video
where specific actions occur.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.720
For example, in a puppy football
game, the action of a dog

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.770
making a touchdown.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.950
Now, object tracking is where
you get labels, bounding boxes,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:48.220
and time stamps for objects
you want to track in a video.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.310
For example, in
the same game, you

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.800
want to track the football's
motion throughout the video.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.970
Now, we just covered the
basic functionality here.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.290
I have included the link below
for more details and best

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:02.480
practices on data preparation.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.990
At this point, I think we want
to see how to create and manage

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:07.860
the data sets in the console.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.740
So let's dive in.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.440
In the console, we get
to Vertex AI and click

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.430
on the data set where we
see image, tabular, text,

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.580
and video data sets for the
different objectives we talked

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.140
about earlier.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.990
Let's try to create
an image data set.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.930
Say we want to do
image classification.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.090
Before we begin,
it's good to check

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:31.790
the Data Guide that
shows us the requirements

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:33.890
and recommendations.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.830
You can import files
directly from your computer,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:39.890
and they would be
stored in Cloud Storage.

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:42.230
We can then add the
corresponding labels

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:44.820
for our images in the next step.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.380
If you already have labels,
use the Import File option

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:53.030
to import a CSV with your
image URLs and their labels.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.060
The entries in the CSV would
look something like this.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.390
If your data is not
labeled, and you

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.640
would like human
help to label it,

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.340
then you could use the
data labeling service.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.410
Let's upload a few files here.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:09.710
Once the files are uploaded,
we can create labels

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:11.920
and assign them to the images.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.820
We can also analyze
the images we

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:17.180
have in the data set,
number of images per label

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.760
and a few other properties.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.430
Depending on the type
of data, your options

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:23.900
might vary slightly.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.660
For example, if you
look at tabular data,

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.910
you could upload a CSV
file from your computer,

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:35.550
from Cloud Storage, or select
a table from BigQuery directly.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:39.990
Once you select the table, the
data is available for analysis.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:45.130
We can see the number of
rows, columns, et cetera.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.080
You would do the same
for a text data set.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.530
Once uploaded, we
can see the labels

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:52.970
or add new labels to the data.

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:56.020
And we can also
analyze a data set.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.990
The video data set
interface looks similar.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:03.670
I have some labeled sports
files here for golf, cartwheel,

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:07.090
horse riding, and others.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:09.460
That was a high
level overview of how

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.760
you can manage different
